@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+# Local patches
+
+This checkout is **not** stock [onivim/libvim](https://github.com/onivim/libvim).
+It carries deliberate changes on top of it, listed below.
+
+libvim is unmaintained upstream, so these live as ordinary commits on `master`
+rather than as a rebasing patch queue — there is nothing left to merge from
+`origin`. If you are building an application against this checkout, read this
+file first: one of the changes removes a feature that upstream libvim
+advertises, and if your application wanted that feature you need to know before
+you build on it.
+
+Each change is also kept as a standalone diff so it can be applied to, or
+reverted from, a pristine libvim tree.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Visual-mode `I` and `A` are vim's operators again, not multiple cursors
+
+**Standalone diff:** `vimango/patches/libvim-visual-block-insert.patch`
+**Touches:** `src/normal.c`, `src/ops.c`, `src/state_machine.c`,
+`src/proto/ops.pro`, `src/proto/state_machine.pro`
+**Tests:** `src/apitest/visual_mode.c`
+
+### What upstream libvim does
+
+In stock vim, `I` and `A` in Visual mode are *operators*: `nv_edit()` hands
+them to `v_visop()`, which turns them into `OP_INSERT` / `OP_APPEND`, which
+`do_pending_operator()` routes to `op_insert()` in ops.c. For a blockwise
+selection `op_insert()` runs `edit()` on the first line and then replicates
+the text you typed onto every other line of the block. That is what makes
+`ctrl-v jjjI- <esc>` prefix four lines with `- `.
+
+libvim replaced that with a multiple-cursor model aimed at Onivim 2. Its
+`nv_edit()` computes one position per line of the block, reports each through
+the `vimSetCursorAddCallback()` hook, ends Visual mode, and then performs a
+single ordinary insert at the cursor. The replication is left to the embedder,
+which is expected to maintain real multiple cursors and apply the typed text at
+each of them.
+
+That is a reasonable design for a GUI editor with a multi-cursor UI. It is a
+trap for anything else: an embedder that does not register the callback — and
+registering it is optional — silently gets a plain single-line insert with no
+error, no beep, and no indication that the block was dropped. libvim's own test
+suite recorded the gap: `test_insert_block_mode_change` in
+`apitest/visual_mode.c` was committed commented out, with the note "this test
+fails and will be commented out".
+
+Non-blockwise Visual `I`/`A` were affected too, and more bluntly: in the
+non-block branch of `nv_edit()` the `v_visop()` call was simply commented out,
+so charwise and linewise `I`/`A` also fell through to a plain insert at the
+cursor rather than at the start/end of the selection.
+
+### What this checkout does
+
+Restores upstream vim's behavior. `nv_edit()` calls `v_visop(cap)` for Visual
+`I`/`A` — block and non-block alike — and the multiple-cursor path is gone.
+
+Restoring that one call is necessary but not sufficient, because of how libvim
+reworked insert mode. libvim's `edit()` cannot block waiting for keystrokes; it
+was refactored into a pushdown state machine (`src/state_machine.c`) whose
+states are driven one key at a time by `vimInput()`/`vimKey()`. Any operator
+that needs to run code *after* Insert mode ends must therefore split itself in
+two and put that code in a cleanup callback. libvim had already done this for
+the change operator — `op_change()` became
+`state_change_initialize` / `state_change_execute` / `state_change_cleanup`,
+with the blockwise replication in the cleanup half — which is why blockwise `c`
+worked in stock libvim while blockwise `I` did not.
+
+`op_insert()` never got that treatment. It still called `edit()` inline and did
+its replication immediately afterwards, which under the state machine meant it
+could not work at all; nothing called it, so nobody noticed.
+
+So this change also converts it, exactly parallel to the change operator:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| `state_insert_initialize(oap, count1)` | everything `op_insert()` did before `edit()`; ends by creating the nested edit state |
+| `state_insert_execute(ctx, c)` | forwards each key to the nested edit state |
+| `state_insert_cleanup(ctx)` | everything `op_insert()` did after `edit()` returned — the `block_insert()` replication |
+
+State carried across the split lives in `insertState_T` (`oap`, the
+`block_def`, `pre_textlen`, `ind_pre`, `t1`). The two `u_save_cursor() == FAIL`
+bail-outs leave `editContext` NULL, which makes execute pop the state
+immediately and cleanup skip the replication, instead of running on a
+half-initialized context.
+
+`do_pending_operator()`'s `OP_INSERT` / `OP_APPEND` case now pushes that state
+via `sm_push_insert_op()` and returns, mirroring the `OP_CHANGE` case —
+including its `finish_op = FALSE` and `restart_edit = 0`. The `restart_edit = 0`
+is load-bearing: without it, `state_normal_cmd_execute()` pushes a *second*
+insert state on top of the one just pushed.
+
+One upstream call was intentionally dropped: `auto_format(FALSE, TRUE)` after
+the insert, which only matters with `'formatoptions'` containing `a`. libvim's
+`op_change()` conversion dropped the equivalent call, and this follows it.
+
+### If you want multiple cursors
+
+Revert this change. `vimSetCursorAddCallback()` and the `cursorAddCallback`
+global still exist and are still wired up in `libvim.c` — nothing calls the
+callback any more for a blockwise `I`/`A`, that is all. The six disabled tests
+in `apitest/cursor.c` (`test_add_cursors_visual_I` and friends, kept behind
+`#if 0`) are the specification of what the callback used to report; they are
+the fastest way back if you need it.
+
+The two behaviors are mutually exclusive, not additive: if libvim replicates
+the text itself *and* an embedder applies it at each reported cursor, every
+line but the first gets it twice.
+
+### Verifying
+
+```sh
+cd src
+make libvim.a
+make apitest/visual_mode.test.exe
+cd apitest && ./visual_mode.test.exe
+```
+
+`apitest/visual_mode.c` covers block `I`, block `A`, block `$A`, and a line too
+short to reach the block's start column (`I` skips it, `A` pads it with
+spaces). `test_insert_block_mode_change` is upstream's own disabled test,
+re-enabled — its expectations were right all along, it just needed the two
+`vimInput("<c-v>")`/`vimInput("<esc>")` calls corrected to `vimKey()`, since
+`vimInput()` passes its argument verbatim rather than parsing termcodes.
+
+Expectations throughout were taken from stock vim 9.1, which is worth doing for
+anything in this area:
+
+```sh
+printf 'alpha\nbravo\ncharlie\ndelta\n' > /tmp/f.txt
+vim -u NONE -N -es -c 'exe "normal! gg0\<C-v>jjI- \<esc>"' -c 'wq!' /tmp/f.txt
+cat /tmp/f.txt
+```
+
+### Downstream
+
+Built for [vimango](https://github.com/slzatz/vimango), which embeds libvim as
+a static `libvim.a` and wants stock vim keybindings rather than a multi-cursor
+editor. Its `patches/` directory holds this diff, and its CLAUDE.md tells its
+own build process to apply it. Nothing here is vimango-specific — the result is
+just closer to vim than upstream libvim is.
